[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 11:12] I don't have elasticity or competitor pricing data. Beyond pricing, what else could Brew and Bean do to reverse the margin decline? Give me a structured set.

[candidate · 12:40] Three buckets.

One, pricing — phased increases, and vary by market rather than one flat national hike.

Two, input costs. Hedge or lock in bean contracts so a 40% swing doesn't flow straight through. Renegotiate dairy and packaging with volume commitments. Reformulate portion sizes slightly on the highest-cost drinks.

Three, mix and overhead. Push customers toward higher-margin items — food, larger sizes — where the bean cost per dollar is lower. And overhead went 12 to 14 points, which is worth a look, especially if the 15% revenue growth came from new stores that aren't carrying their own overhead yet.

I'd also want to separate same-store from new-store performance before committing to any of this. Do we have that split?
