[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 17:09] Give me your recommendation to the CEO.

[candidate · 20:16] Raise prices — Brew & Bean's margin collapse is an input-cost pass-through problem, not a demand problem.

The case: COGS went from 42% to 58% of revenue, which is 16 of the 18 points lost. Beans are up 40%, other inputs 37.5%, and menu prices haven't moved in two years. Meanwhile same-store volume is up 15% on a flat 200-store footprint, so customers aren't the issue — they're absorbing more coffee at prices we never adjusted.

What I'd do: a phased increase, roughly 10% over two or three steps, which by my math takes margin from 6% to about 14-15%. Alongside that, lock in bean supply with forward contracts so the next swing doesn't hit us raw, and renegotiate dairy and packaging using the higher volume as leverage.

Risks: we have no elasticity or competitor pricing data, so 10% could cost more volume than I'm assuming. And I haven't explained the 2-point overhead rise on a flat footprint.

Next steps: price-test in a few markets before national rollout, and audit that overhead line.
